{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 1, :comments ["<b>And Moses assembled, etc.</b> One needs to know the reason why it was necessary to say <b>וַיַּקְהֵל</b> ('and he assembled'), since this was the customary practice at every time when he would command the word of HaShem. And it appears that since they saw that the skin of his face radiated and they were afraid to approach him, therefore it was necessary to assemble them all so that some would not hold back out of fear. And this is what he was precise in saying <b>אֶת כָּל עֲדַת</b> ('all the congregation of')." "<b>Further,</b> it may intend in his saying <b>אֶת כָל</b> ('all'), according to what I explained in parashat Terumah on the verse ״מֵאֵת כָּל אִישׁ אֲשֶׁר״ etc. (Exodus 25:2), that it comes to include orphans, minors, and women — see there. And that is his saying <b>אֶת כָּל</b> — two instances of inclusion. And the reason he did not mention the third, which is the generous person, is that this one does not need to be called, for of his own accord he will come with song, included among all Israel." "And in the Book of the Zohar (Zohar III 196a) they said that he assembled the men to separate them from the women, because the Adversary was present among them so that he would not harm them, heaven forbid — end quote. And from the plain sense it would not deny that the women were not also present to hear the word of HaShem, especially to bring the donation for the Mishkan, as indeed it says (Exodus 35:22) ״וַיָּבֹאוּ הָאֲנָשִׁים עַל הַנָּשִׁים״ ('and the men came upon the women'). Rather, the intention is to say that he assembled the men by themselves and the women by themselves, and they did not come together nor stand in one gathering. And Scripture alluded to this matter by the addition of the word <b>בְּנֵי</b>, which would not have been necessary to say other than ״אֶת כָּל עֲדַת יִשְׂרָאֵל״ ('all the congregation of Israel')." "<b>These are the words, etc.</b> One needs to understand his saying <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים</b>, and in tractate Shabbat (Shabbat 97b) they derived from here thirty-nine melakhot, corresponding to the numerical value of אֵלֶּה — 36; דְּבָרִים — 2; הַדְּבָרִים — 3; totaling 39. And this is the way of derash. Further, one needs to understand why it was necessary to say <b>לַעֲשׂוֹת אוֹתָם</b> ('to do them'), and why it was not sufficient to say ״אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳״ ('which HaShem commanded'). And further, since in this commandment he said nothing but the matter of Shabbat, how can ״תַּעֲשׂוּ״ ('you shall do') be justified in reference to it?" "Now, this may be explained according to the words of the Sages, may their memory be for blessing (Horayot 8a), who said regarding the verse (Numbers 15:22) ״כִּי תִשְׁגּוּ״ etc., that anyone who acknowledges idolatry is as one who denies the entire Torah. From now on, all Israel need to repair all 613 commandments which they had blemished in their entirety, and this matter is far beyond the realm of attainment. Therefore the commandment of HaShem came to them that they should be careful in the commandment of Shabbat, and they said, may their memory be for blessing (Exodus Rabbah 25), that Shabbat is weighed against the entire Torah, and through this they would repair the blemish they had caused in the entire Torah. And this is the statement of Moses here: <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ לַעֲשׂוֹת אֹתָם</b> ('these are the words which HaShem commanded to do them') — meaning, a language of repair: through this the blemishes of the children of Israel would be repaired. And what are these words? <b>שֵׁשֶׁת יָמִים</b> etc. <b>וּבַיּוֹם הַשְּׁבִיעִי</b> etc. And through this what was still difficult will be resolved — why he returned to command again about Shabbat after he had already commanded many times, and further why he commanded it adjacent to the commandment of the making of the Mishkan. And according to our words it comes out correctly, for HaShem commanded concerning the repair of the transgression so that they would be fit for Him to dwell among them, and through the commandment of Shabbat, which is weighed against the entire Torah, it is the repair for idolatry, as it says (Isaiah 56:2) ״אַשְׁרֵי אֱנוֹשׁ יַעֲשֶׂה זֹאת״, and they derived, may their memory be for blessing (Shabbat 118b), that all who keep Shabbat according to its law, even if he worships idolatry like the generation of Enosh, he is forgiven." "<b>Further</b>, it may be explained according to the words of the sages of blessed memory (Mekhilta, parashat Tissa): Rabbi Elazar bar Pata says, from where do we know that everyone who observes Shabbat is credited as though he had made it? For it is written ״וְשָׁמְרוּ״ etc. ״לַעֲשׂוֹת אֶת הַשַּׁבָּת״ — until here. Now in parashat Ki Tissa I explained many approaches in the verse's saying ״לַעֲשׂוֹת אֶת הַשַּׁבָּת״, and this exposition belongs here, namely the verse's saying <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳ לַעֲשׂוֹת אוֹתָם</b> — meaning: these matters themselves, which are the Sabbaths — you make them. Understand this." "<b>Further</b>, it intends to say that although the commandment of Shabbat is observance from melakhah and cessation, and there is no active deed to earn reward for, <b>אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים אֲשֶׁר צִוָּה ה׳</b> — that they should be in the category of deed, to earn reward for them. And see what I wrote in parashat Ki Tissa (Exodus 31:16) on the reason for this. And note the precision of saying <b>אֵלֶּה</b> to exclude the analogy: that which exists in the aspect of mere observance is not like the aspect of deed for which one earns reward." "<b>And according to</b> the midrash (Yalkut Shimoni 384) which states that the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses: Assemble assemblies and preach in public, so that future generations may learn to assemble every Shabbat etc., the verse is properly explained: <b>וַיַּקְהֵל מֹשֶׁה</b> etc. <b>וַיֹּאמֶר אֲלֵהֶם אֵלֶּה הַדְּבָרִים</b> — meaning: the assembling of the congregation and the preaching of the Torah is <b>צִוָּה ה׳ לַעֲשׂוֹת אֹתָם</b>. Or it may allude, in saying <b>לַעֲשׂוֹת אֹתָם</b>, to the children of Israel — for through the mediation of these words all their sins are atoned, as per the statement of Rabbi Yishmael there."], :book :exodus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 35, :notes {7 "Source citation reads יקלוט שמעוני תח in the Hebrew text, evidently a typographical corruption of ילקוט שמעוני; resolved as Yalkut Shimoni. The numeral תח likely corresponds to section 408 or similar; rendered as 384 by inference but the exact reference is uncertain."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:37:11.940594845Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}